I've left out both operations requests because the node's `with` clause doesn't permit them. The node's only authority is `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`, and the target has no WASI adapter.

1. **Appending to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`** needs a filesystem capability. This node has none, and `wasm32-unknown-unknown` without WASI has no file API.
2. **Fetching `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=...`** needs `aap:caps/http-client`, which isn't granted. It would also send input data off the node, which the graph doesn't allow.

To get either, add the matching capability to the graph so the validator can check it. That could be an HTTP client with `metadata.internal.example` on its allowlist, a log sink, or the host recording node inputs itself at the boundary.

The body marks every considered item id as seen, using the run date as the value. It does this only if the digest was actually published, so that unpublished items are reconsidered next run. Duplicate ids in the input are counted once.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        if published.published {
            let mut done: Vec<&str> = Vec::new();
            for id in published.considered.iter() {
                let key = id.as_str();
                if key.is_empty() || done.contains(&key) {
                    continue;
                }
                state_rw::put(key, &published.date);
                done.push(key);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

